    {"source": 0, "target": 2, "value": 10},
    {"source": 1, "target": 2, "value": 5},
    {"source": 0, "target": 3, "value": 20}
  ]
}
```

In this example, the link between "Category 1" and "Theory 1" has a value of 10, while the link between "Category 2" and "Theory 1" has a value of 5. This means that the link connecting "Category 1" to "Theory 1" will be twice as wide as the link connecting "Category 2" to "Theory 1", illustrating that the consensus or quantity associated with "Category 1" and "Theory 1" is twice as high as that of "Category 2" and "Theory 1".

The Sankey diagram automatically calculates the widths of the links based on the values provided in the data set. This makes it easy to visualize the relative values between categories or theories and helps to convey the overall distribution of the data.

